{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "transforms",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "The frames of a robot as one tree: each transform places a child frame in its parent frame, and the transforms compose along the tree to place any frame of it in any other at a time. transform_lookup answers such a lookup for a node that holds no tree.",
      "Frames. A frame is a link of the robot's model, named byte for byte as in the URDF that robot_identity's get_description gives.",
      "Robot frame. The root link of the URDF is the robot frame. It is fixed to the robot's base: its origin is the point the base stands on, +X points the way the robot faces, +Y to its left and +Z up. It moves with the robot, and it is not a frame of the room or of a simulated world. On a robot with a mobile base the root link is base_footprint. Every contract that places a position or a pose in the robot frame means this frame.",
      "Transforms. A transform has a parent frame and a child frame, distinct and not empty, and places the child frame in the parent frame: a point p of the child frame is R(q) * p + t of the parent frame, with t the transform's position, x, y, z in meters, and q its orientation, a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]. Each frame has at most one parent at a time, and no frame is its own ancestor, so the transforms form a tree. Two frames whose chains of parents share no frame have no lookup between them. A consumer drops a transform whose frames are empty or equal, whose numbers are not all finite, or whose quaternion's squared norm lies 0.01 or more from 1, the tolerance of tf2's buffer, and normalizes the quaternion of each transform it keeps. It drops a message whose arrays do not hold one parent frame, 3 position entries and 4 orientation entries per child frame, or that names a child frame twice.",
      "Joints. The transform of a joint of the model runs from the joint's parent link to its child link: the joint's origin in the URDF, then its motion at its position: a rotation by the angle about the joint's axis for a revolute or a continuous joint, a translation by the distance along its axis for a prismatic joint, the pose of the child link for a floating or a planar joint, and none for a fixed joint. A mimic joint takes the position its mimic element gives from the joint it follows.",
      "Publishers. Each transform has one publisher. The implementer of robot_identity publishes the transform of every fixed joint of the model on static_transforms, from the URDF it holds; these transforms are the robot's design: exact in a simulation, nominal on hardware, where a part stands within the tolerance of its mount. The node that measures a moving joint publishes the joint's transform on transforms at its measured position, and the transform of every mimic joint that follows it at the same timestamp; a moving joint that no node measures and that follows no measured joint has no transform, and the links below it have no place in the tree. An implementer publishes nothing on a topic that carries none of its transforms.",
      "Time. A transforms carries the transforms its publisher measured at its timestamp, one timestamp for all of them, as limb_states carries one for its joints; a publisher whose transforms are measured at different times publishes a message for each time. Timestamps are on the clock the implementer's instance reads, the simulation clock in a simulation, and every publisher of a robot's tree stamps on one clock, since a lookup composes the transforms of several publishers at one time. A static transform holds at every time. When a stamp of a moving transform arrives, a consumer drops the stamps of its child frame older than the child frame's newest held stamp less a cache window (s, finite and above 0) that its caller or its launcher sets, then takes the arriving stamp unless it is older than that too, as tf2's buffer does; an arriving stamp equal to a held one replaces it. When the clock its instance reads goes back, a consumer drops the stamps of every moving transform it holds and keeps its static transforms, which hold at every time.",
      "Starting over. A transform starts over when its value stops following from its stamps before, and every transform of a publisher starts over when the publisher starts. Each transforms gives in start_timestamp when its transforms last started over, on the wall clock of the machine that runs its publisher, so a publisher whose transforms start over at different times sends them in separate messages. A publisher is the instance that publishes, named by its core node and its instance id. A consumer that receives from a publisher a start of the transform of a child frame other than the start of the stamps of that child frame it holds from that publisher drops those stamps, so no lookup interpolates across a start. Two starts are compared for equality alone.",
      "Lookups. The place of a source frame in a target frame at a time composes the transforms on the chain from each frame up to their nearest common ancestor. A moving transform at a time between its nearest stamp before that time and its nearest stamp after it is interpolated between the two, linearly in position and spherically along the shorter arc in orientation, as tf2 interpolates; where the two stamps give the child frame different parents, the transform takes the older stamp's, as tf2 does. A moving transform has no value before its oldest held stamp or after its newest, and a lookup that needs one fails, naming the transform, the time and the stamps held. A moving transform of which the consumer holds no stamp has no value at any time and gives its child frame no parent at the latest time, as tf2 does. A lookup at the latest time takes the oldest of the newest stamps of the moving transforms on the chain that the newest stamp of each frame gives, as tf2 takes it. A frame looked up in itself stands at the origin, whether or not a transform names it. Transforms from two publishers to one child frame break the tree at that frame while the consumer holds a transform of it from each: a moving transform while one of its stamps lies within the cache window counted back from the newest stamp of that frame from either publisher, and a static transform until its publisher's next static_transforms leaves it out. A lookup whose chain needs the transform from such a frame to its parent fails, naming the frame and both publishers, and one whose chain runs through a cycle fails, naming every transform of the cycle. A lookup that fails gives a frame named by no transform the consumer received that passed the checks of Transforms first, the target frame's before the source frame's; then the first failure its walks up the two chains meet, a broken tree or a missing value, the source frame's walk before the target frame's, as tf2's walks meet them; then two frames with no chain between them.",
      "No member has a mark.",
    ],
  },
  interfaces: {
    topics: [
      {
        name: "transforms",
        description: "Moving transforms that one publisher measured at timestamp: some or all of the moving transforms it owns, as the description of this contract assigns them. A publisher publishes one at each of its measurements.",
        // High-frequency stream; a lookup interpolates across a dropped message,
        // and every message carries the starts of its transforms.
        qos_profile: "sensor_data",
        message_format: {
          timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "When the publisher measured every transform of this message." },
          child_frames: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The child frame of each transform, each named once. parent_frames, positions and orientations are index-aligned with this array." },
          parent_frames: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The parent frame of each transform, in child_frames order." },
          positions: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "3 entries per transform (x, y, z, m): the child frame's origin in the parent frame, in child_frames order." },
          orientations: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "4 entries per transform, a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]: the child frame's orientation in the parent frame, in child_frames order." },
          start_timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "When every transform of this message last started over, on the wall clock of the machine that runs its publisher: a new value means they started over." },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "static_transforms",
        description: "The transform of every fixed joint of the model, each holding at every time. The implementer of robot_identity publishes one once it holds the model, and the newest replaces the one before.",
        // A consumer needs every fixed transform the moment it subscribes, and
        // each message replaces the one before it.
        qos_profile: "reliable",
        retention: { latest: 1 },
        message_format: {
          child_frames: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The child frame of each transform, each named once. parent_frames, positions and orientations are index-aligned with this array." },
          parent_frames: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The parent frame of each transform, in child_frames order." },
          positions: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "3 entries per transform (x, y, z, m): the child frame's origin in the parent frame, in child_frames order." },
          orientations: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "4 entries per transform, a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]: the child frame's orientation in the parent frame, in child_frames order." },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
}
